00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:07.010 align:start position:0%
the 737 max is the fastest selling plane  in Boeing's history but ever since it  was grounded in March its future has  been uncertain the crash of a lion air  jet in Indonesia last October with the  loss of a hundred and eighty nine lives  raised questions about the plane's  flight control systems when an Ethiopian  Airlines plane went down five months  later another 157 people died and  regulators ordered the planes be removed  from service since then Boeing's  continued production even though the  planes delivery to Airlines is frozen  with them grounded well into next year  the company's now called a temporary  halt to production suspending production  of a commercial aircraft is something  that has been very rarely done it truly  is an unprecedented decision they don't  have all the problems identified and  fixed yet and I think that they are wise  to consider pausing production for one  thing I don't know physically where they  can store these planes last week  relatives of some of those who died in  the crashes were at a congressional  hearing in Washington where a retired  Boeing production manager said he told  the company that factory conditions were  jeopardizing aircraft safety I formally  warned Boeing leadership in writing on  multiple occasions specifically once  before the lion air crashed and again  before the Ethiopian Airlines crash  about potential airplane risk due to the  unstable operating environment within  the factory those warnings were ignored  at the same hearing the head of the  Federal Aviation Administration refused  to set a timeline for the plane to  return to the skies when the 737 max has  returned to service it will be because  the safety issues have been addressed  and pilots have received all the  training they need to safely operate the  aircraft this process is not guided by a  calendar or schedule it's not clear how  the suspension will affect the 12,000  workers on the production line or the  financial implications the grounding of  the 737 max has already cost Boeing  billions of dollars  what price though safety and the lives  of those who've been lost  Greg Milam Sky News Los Angeles
 

